**The engine calls the mod's Lua to run trade, and it does so on a worker thread.** One caller, 0x27B1A2, inside 0x27B160, which is the per-country body of ProcessAITradeFunctor - and FINDINGS-tick has that functor fanned out from RunHourlyPass at 0x6829DC, every hour. So this is **once per country per hour, on a TBB worker thread**.

It draws MT19937Next (0x6A2B90) directly against the game state's generator at +0x124 - not through Random (0x6A2F80) - and:

    if (draw % 6 == 0)        Lua ProposeTrades(country->ai (+0x1D8), country->tag (+0xCA4))
    else if (draw % 10 == 0)  Lua EvalutateExistingTrades(country->ai, country->tag)

The bridge is 0x110280 with the function name in EAX and a result buffer in ESI. Both names are BlackICE's own, in `script/ai_trade.lua` (`EvalutateExistingTrades` is misspelled in the engine's literal too, so the mod's spelling is required). **So the AI's trade-route decisions are entirely the mod's**, and a script error there silently stops the AI trading.

Where a trade route's goods actually move - **not** CConvoy::RunDelivery, which skips every convoy with is_trade set. Called from RunCountryDailyPass (0xDA530) at 0x4DB43A. It walks all countries out of the CCountryDataBase (+0x168 count, +0x16C vector), skips "REB", takes `country->diplomacy_status (+0xE28)[otherId]` and its trade-route list at +0x60 (count +0x68), and for each route:

- if the serving convoy has no transports (+0x98 == 0) the route is inactive, with the reason string TRADE_INACTIVE_CONVOY;
- if the seller has none of the good, TRADE_INACTIVE_NO_RES;
- otherwise CConvoy::GetEfficiency (called at 0xFF380) scales the amount, and the two sides' pools are credited: `TradedAway` (CCountry +0x7DC, so +0x7E4 + good*4), `TradedFor` (+0x86C, so +0x874 + good*4) and `TradedAwaySansAlliedSupply` (+0x800, so +0x808 + good*4), with the roles swapped for the other direction.

**So an escorted-away trade convoy stops the trade outright rather than reducing it**, and convoy efficiency is a straight multiplier on everything else.

**Decides BM_TERRAIN (0x19) and nothing else.** `ret 8`, no direct callers - slot 20 of CCombatant, and although it is on the shared base **only the land slot 19 calls it** (0x16A960), so BM_TERRAIN is land-only in practice.

    terrain = this->combat(+0x3C)->terrain(+0x24)
    adj     = unit->+0xC8->terrain_adjusters(+0x54)[terrain->id(+0x8)]      ; 0x18 bytes each
    if is_attacker(+0x38): v = terrain->attack(+0x50)  + adj->attack(+0x8)
    else:                  v = terrain->defence(+0x4C) + adj->defence(+0xC)
    if v != 0: this->+0x21 = 1; CUnit::AddCombatModifier(unit, BM_TERRAIN, v, v)

The index/stride are `lea eax,[eax+eax*2]` then `lea eax,[edi+eax*8]` = 24 bytes, which confirms CSubUnitDefinition.terrain_adjusters is indexed by terrain id. **No define enters at all.** Because unit->+0xC8 is the division's own summed CSubUnitDefinition, the adjuster term is the sum over the division's brigades. Signature corrected 2026-10-02 by the signature audit (reversing/findings/FINDINGS-sigaudit.md).

0x495140 to the exits at 0x495394 / 0x4953AE (true) and 0x495524 (false) - bare `ret` with four stack arguments, so __cdecl. Read end to end; the name is inference. Five callers: CAIUnit::SetArea at 0x4BA8B8, two in slot 79 (0x4B3480, 0x4B37DB) and two at 0x498923/0x4989F3. CAIUnit::SetArea calls it as `0x495140(ourCountry, ownerArea, &out, 0)` at pass B, and the out-byte is what makes the agent widen its front search past its own theatre.

False unless the area is valid (slot 0), its controller resolves (COwnerArea +0x24's first province, tag at +0x334 / id at +0x338; `---`/0 when +0x2C is zero) and is not REB. Then it needs CCountry::IsSameSide, or else military access (CDiplomacyStatus +0x58) **and** not CCountry::IsEnemyTag. After that:
- `us->government_in_exile` (+0x95) short-circuits to **true**;
- otherwise, if `us->ai` (+0x1D8) exists and is an AI-run country (CEU3AI +0x2C), it walks the AI's focus province list at CEU3AI +0x118: a focus province inside this area, or in a neighbour area (COwnerArea +0x34) whose owner we are at war with, sets **`*out = true`** and returns true;
- then: false if the controller is human-played (gamestate->played_countries_array);
- true if `acceptAnyUnplayed`;
- true if the controller's faction_leader_id (CCountry +0xF3C) is our own id (+0xCA8), i.e. we lead their faction;
- otherwise true if some neighbour area of more than five provinces belongs to someone we are at war with and either `gamestate->scenario` (+0xD0C) is set or the war's own attacker/defender vectors say the leading enemy is not human-played.

So the out-parameter and the return value answer two different questions: `*out` is 'this is one of my own focus areas', the return value is 'I am allowed to look for a front here'.

0x4BE1C0 to the `ret 4` at 0x4BE2BA - 250 bytes, read end to end, int3 padding then a new function at 0x4BE2C0. The receiver arrives in **EDI** and is never loaded from the frame, so it is not a __thiscall; the single stack argument is a byte. Ten callers, two of them inside CAIUnit slot 83 (0x4C0AFE, 0x4C0C7A), the rest in 0x4B8178 and the 0x4C5E95-0x4C7244 cluster.

    if (preferAiTarget && unit->vf[15]() && unit->vf[9]()->ai_param_a != 0)
        return gamestate->provinces_begin[ unit->army()->ai_param_a ];
    p = CUnit::GetMovementDestinationProvince(unit);
    return p ? p : unit->current_province_ptr;

Slot 15 is the land predicate (ReturnTrue only on CArmy, ReturnFalse on CUnit/CNavy/CAir) and slot 9 is CArmy's `mov eax,ecx; ret`, so the first arm is reachable only for an army. **It is the one place that shows what CArmy +0x2E8 (`ai_param_a`) is: a province id**, used as the index into CGameState +0xB8C. So the function answers 'the province this unit is anchored on' - the AI's target if one is set, else where it is marching, else where it stands.

**Slot 84**, 0x4C1790 to the bare `ret` at 0x4C1C8E; the `ret` at 0x4C18BB is an early exit, not the end. It hands the agent's objectives down to its subordinate armies:

1. collect this unit's land children that themselves have children (CUnit +0x1EC > 0), clearing each one's CArmy +0x2EC as it goes;
2. for every objective on this agent's own CArmy +0x2EC whose `invasion` (+0x10) is **not** > 0, give a copy to the subordinate minimising `0x4C1C90(sub, this, objective->province) + 20.0 * sub->objectiveCount` - so the second term is a load-balancing penalty;
3. any subordinate still holding nothing gets a copy of its own nearest objective;
4. for each subordinate, if its plan's objective count (CUnit +0x23C) differs from the computed count, or the province sets differ, post `CSetPlanObjectivesCommand(sub, &sub->+0x2EC)` (ctor 0x1E68B0, 0x74 bytes);
5. free each CArmy +0x2EC again. Answers whether anything was posted.

**It ignores CObjective::priority entirely** - it reads only `province` and `invasion`. Each copy it makes writes the vftable 0x15C8BE0 by hand at 0x4C198E and 0x4C1AB0 rather than calling CopyObjectiveList.

**The real `basic_stringbuf<char>::str()`, and its address is rva `0x520940` - not `0x920940`.** `0x920940` is the *virtual* address the disassembler prints inside `std::ostringstream::str` (`0x65BD10`); the handover carried it across as an rva. Rva `0x920940` (VA `0xD20940`) is not a function entry at all: it decodes from the middle of an instruction and has zero callers. This is the trap `findings/README.md` warns about, and the previous wave's own `frontier` list did record it correctly as `0x520940`.

The body is MSVC's `str()` unchanged:

    if (!(_Mystate & _Constant) && pptr() != 0)
        return string(pbase(), max(_Seekhigh, pptr()) - pbase());
    else if (!(_Mystate & _Noread) && gptr() != 0)
        return string(eback(), egptr() - eback());
    else
        return string();

All three arms build a local `Hoi3CString` with `std::string::assign` (`0xA160`), tidy `out` to the empty state (`[out+0x14]=0xF`, `[out+0x10]=0`, `[out]=0`) and copy the local into it with `0x32720`, then free the local when its capacity reached 0x10.

**Field offsets, streambuf-relative** (MSVC keeps pointers *to* the area pointers, so each is dereferenced twice): `+0x10` `_IGfirst`, `+0x14` `_IPfirst`, `+0x20` `_IGnext`, `+0x24` `_IPnext`, `+0x30` `_IGcount`; `egptr()` is computed as `*_IGnext + *_IGcount`. `+0x3C` is `_Seekhigh` and `+0x40` `_Mystate`, which is the cross-check that settles the identification: `LogRecord` puts its stringbuf at `record+4` and the already-confirmed `std::ostringstream::ostringstream` writes `_Seekhigh` at `record+0x40` and `_Mystate` at `record+0x44` - the same two fields, the same two offsets.

**Convention.** `esi` is never pushed, never assigned and never restored, and the epilogue is `mov eax,esi` - so it is an incoming argument, the named-return-value slot. `ecx` is the buffer. Bare `ret`, nothing on the stack. Both callers set it up that way: `std::ostringstream::str` does `mov esi,eax; add ecx,4; call` and the second caller, `0x51FB50`, does `mov esi,eax; add ecx,0x18; call`.

The state bits are `_Constant = 2` and `_Noread = 4`, tested in that order.

**`std::basic_ostream<char>::write(const char*, streamsize)`, verbatim.** The body is the documented implementation instruction for instruction:

    ios_base::iostate _State = goodbit;
    const sentry _Ok(*this);                       // sentry_base + sentry
    if (!_Ok) _State |= badbit;
    else if (rdbuf()->sputn(_Str, _Count) != _Count) _State |= badbit;
    setstate(_State);
    return *this;

Each piece is identifiable. `sentry_base`'s constructor is `if (rdbuf()) rdbuf()->_Lock()` - the virtual call through `[rdbuf]+4` at `0xA64A6E`; its destructor is `_Unlock`, slot `+8`, at `0xA64B64`. The `sentry` constructor's `if (good() && tie()) tie()->flush()` is the `[+0xC] == 0` / `[+0x3C] != 0` pair calling `std::ostream::flush` (`0x65BB20`), and `_Ok = good()` is the `sete cl` at `0xA64A96`. The insert is `std::streambuf::sputn` (`0x22170`) with `this` in `ecx` from `basic_ios::_Mystrbuf` at `+0x38`; because `streamsize` is a 64-bit type the return comes back in `edx:eax` and the short-write test is the two compares at `0xA64AC7`. `setstate` is inlined as `clear(rdstate() | _State | (rdbuf() ? 0 : badbit), false)` through `std::ios_base::clear` (`0x20B0`). The `sentry` destructor is `if (!uncaught_exception()) _Osfx()` - `0x794AE9` then `std::ostream::_Osfx` (`0xA260`). The block at `0xA64ADF` is the SEH unwind funclet for the same `setstate`, reached through the scope table at `0xC214FA`.

**It is `write` and not `_Insert_string`**: nothing in the body reads `ios_base::_Wide` (`+0x20/+0x24`) or `_Fillch` (`+0x40`), and there is no closing `width(0)`. `0x21D30` (`std::operator<<Chars`) is the one that does all three.

**The convention is again optimiser-chosen**: `this` is pushed as the first stack argument rather than passed in `ecx`, and the function cleans all 16 bytes itself - `ret 0x10` = stream + pointer + the two halves of the `streamsize`. 352 sites call it directly with a constant folded length; `0x664A00` calls it for the rest.

The `basic_ios` it works on is reached through the virtual base: `[[os]+4] + os`. Fields touched: `+0x0C` `_Mystate`, `+0x38` `_Mystrbuf`, `+0x3C` `_Tiestr`.

Confirms the handover. `ios_base::_Callfns(event)`: walk the singly linked callback list whose head is `_Calls` at `+0x2C` and call each node's function with the event, the stream and the node's index.

    for (node = this->_Calls; node; node = node->_Next)
        node->_Pfn(event, *this, node->_Index);

The node layout falls straight out of the loop - `+0` `_Next`, `+4` `_Index`, `+8` `_Pfn` - and the callback is `__cdecl`: three pushes and `add esp, 0xC` at `0xB94B08`, in the order `(event, this, index)`. That is `typedef void (__cdecl *event_callback)(event, ios_base&, int)` exactly.

`__thiscall`, one stack argument, `ret 4`, nothing returned. **It has exactly one caller in the whole image**, `std::ios_base::_Tidy` (`0x794B15`), which passes the literal 0 - `erase_event`. So no registered callback in this game is ever told about `copyfmt_event` or `imbue_event`; the list is only ever run at teardown.

Confirms the handover. `_Lockit::_Lockit(int _Kind)`: store the kind at `+0`, and if it is below 4 enter the critical section at `0x174C878 + kind*0x18`.

    _Locktype = kind;
    if (kind < _MAX_LOCK) EnterCriticalSection(&mtx[kind]);

The table is four `CRITICAL_SECTION`s - the `0x18` stride is `sizeof(RTL_CRITICAL_SECTION)` on 32 bit - and `0xB958D8`, the thing it calls, is a one-line forwarder to the `EnterCriticalSection` import. `__thiscall`, `ret 4`, the object back in eax (constructors do). 47 callers, paired one for one with 47 callers of the destructor below.

The lock ids seen in use are `0` from `std::locale::Destruct` (`_LOCK_LOCALE`) and `2` from `std::ios_base::_Addstd` (`_LOCK_STREAM`).

Builds one live CFixedWindow from a declared child-window name. This is what fills the parent window's subwindow map at +0x414, and it is the link that makes the `windowType` keyword and that container one fact.

  - looks `name` up in the gui-type tree embedded at CWindowType +0x150 (slot 1 on the embedded object);
  - if absent, asks `[this+0x14C]` (the CGui) for a default through slot 18, passing the type's own `parent` string at +0x68, and re-looks-up under that name;
  - calls the window factory 0xAC4FE0 with the resolved CWindowType in ESI, which does `operator new(0x4C8)` and CFixedWindow::CFixedWindow - so **a subwindow is a full CFixedWindow**;
  - adjusts the result to its CGuiObject with `lea ebx, [esi + 0x18]` and registers it with the CGui through slot 16;
  - returns the **complete** window pointer.

`ret 0x24`: the name is an Hoi3CString **by value** (0x1C bytes at [ebp+8]..[ebp+0x20]) plus two dwords at [ebp+0x24] and [ebp+0x28], which the factory passes straight through. Receiver in ecx. Two callers: 0xAC0C73, the constructor's subwindow build loop, and 0xAC3C27.

**Slot 11, the base body, and the whole of it.** 0x5D06C0 to the single `ret 0xC` at 0x5D06DD:

    if (this->Evaluate(scope)) ++*passed;
    ++*total;

It calls its **own** slot 6 - `[eax+0x18]`, on `ecx`, not on a child - so it is a second, complete evaluation of the trigger rather than a cached reading of an earlier one. 153 of the 164 trigger classes inherit it.

This confirms the signature `project.json` already records for slot 11, argument for argument.

It abuts CAndTrigger::Evaluate: the `ret 0xC` at 0x5D06DD is followed with **no padding** by 0x5D06E0's prologue, which is why functionStart(0x9D06E0) lands here (trap 2). 12 `int3` before this entry.

**Slot 11 for an OR, which counts as one condition rather than as its children.** 0x5D1230 to two `ret 0xC` at 0x5D125C and 0x5D1267 - one function: the `je 0x9D1265` at 0x5D123E falls into the second exit's tail (trap 3, settled by the jump).

    ++*total;
    for each child: if (child->Evaluate(scope)) { ++*passed; break; }

So it adds exactly 1 to the denominator whatever the block holds, and 1 to the numerator if any branch is true - and it does that by calling the children's **slot 6** (`[eax+0x18]`), not their slot 11. 12 `int3` before the entry.

**Slot 11 for a NOT: count the children, then invert the tally.** 0x5D1380 to the single `ret 0xC` at 0x5D13D0.

    int innerPassed = 0, innerTotal = 0;
    for each child: child->CountEvaluation(scope, &innerPassed, &innerTotal)
    *passed += innerTotal - innerPassed;
    *total  += innerTotal;

So a NOT block reports `m - n` of `m` met - the conditions that failed are the ones the NOT is satisfied by. With no children it adds nothing at all, which is consistent with its Evaluate returning true without being counted.

It forwards nothing: the two counters are its own stack slots at [ebp-8] and [ebp-4]. 8 `int3` before the entry, 13 after.
